Q: Optimize C++ function by value analysis A: <p>First of all, the premise in the question seems to be incorrect. Gcc does seem to be able to do this optimization, under at least some circumstances. For example, when I complete the code enough to compile:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int *bar();
bool foo();

void DoSomething(int);
void DoSomethingElse(int);
void DoYetAnotherThing();

int main() {
  int* ptr = bar();
  bool b = foo();

  if (ptr != nullptr)
    DoSomething(*ptr);
  else if (b)
    DoSomethingElse(*ptr);
  else
    DoYetAnotherThing();

  return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>...and compile it with gcc using <code>-O3</code>, I get:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>&quot;main&quot;:
        push    rbx
        call    &quot;bar()&quot;
        mov     rbx, rax       # save ptr in RBX
        call    &quot;foo()&quot;
        test    rbx, rbx
        je      .L2            # jump if pointer is zero
        mov     edi, DWORD PTR [rbx]
        call    &quot;DoSomething(int)&quot;
.L3:
        xor     eax, eax       # function epilogue
        pop     rbx
        ret
.L2:                        # if(ptr == nullptr) {
        test    al, al               # test the bool retval from foo
        je      .L4                  # jump if it's zero (false)
        mov     eax, DWORD PTR ds:0  # load from absolute address 0, the known value of ptr in this block
        ud2                          # and raise an exception (#UD illegal instruction) in case the zero page was mapped so the load didn't segfault.
.L4:
        call    &quot;DoYetAnotherThing()&quot;
        jmp     .L3
</code></pre>
<p>Note that <code>DoSomethingElse</code> is not mentioned in the assemble code at all. Where it would have been called, we get instead a <a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/ud" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ud2</code> instruction</a>, which intentionally crashes the program if the load from absolute address <code>0</code> didn't already do that.  (Emitting <code>ud2</code> is what GCC chooses to do for some cases of compile-time-visible UB, rather than emitting any further code in a basic block.)</p>
<p><kbd><a href="https://godbolt.org/#g:!((g:!((g:!((h:codeEditor,i:(filename:%271%27,fontScale:14,fontUsePx:%270%27,j:1,lang:c%2B%2B,selection:(endColumn:1,endLineNumber:1,positionColumn:1,positionLineNumber:1,selectionStartColumn:1,selectionStartLineNumber:1,startColumn:1,startLineNumber:1),source:%27int+*bar()%3B%0Abool+foo()%3B%0A%0Avoid+DoSomething(int)%3B%0Avoid+DoSomethingElse(int)%3B%0Avoid+DoYetAnotherThing()%3B%0A%0Aint+main()+%7B%0A++int*+ptr+%3D+bar()%3B%0A++bool+b+%3D+foo()%3B%0A%0A++if+(ptr+!!%3D+nullptr)%0A++++DoSomething(*ptr)%3B%0A++else+if+(b)%0A++++DoSomethingElse(*ptr)%3B%0A++else%0A++++DoYetAnotherThing()%3B%0A++++%0A++return+0%3B%0A%7D%0A%27),l:%275%27,n:%270%27,o:%27C%2B%2B+source+%231%27,t:%270%27)),k:50,l:%274%27,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,s:0,t:%270%27),(g:!((h:compiler,i:(compiler:gsnapshot,filters:(b:%270%27,binary:%271%27,binaryObject:%271%27,commentOnly:%270%27,debugCalls:%271%27,demangle:%270%27,directives:%270%27,execute:%271%27,intel:%270%27,libraryCode:%270%27,trim:%271%27,verboseDemangling:%270%27),flagsViewOpen:%271%27,fontScale:14,fontUsePx:%270%27,j:1,lang:c%2B%2B,libs:!(),options:%27-std%3Dc%2B%2B26+-O3+-Wall+-Wextra%27,overrides:!(),selection:(endColumn:1,endLineNumber:1,positionColumn:1,positionLineNumber:1,selectionStartColumn:1,selectionStartLineNumber:1,startColumn:1,startLineNumber:1),source:1),l:%275%27,n:%270%27,o:%27+x86-64+gcc+(trunk)+(Editor+%231)%27,t:%270%27)),k:50,l:%274%27,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,s:0,t:%270%27)),l:%272%27,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,t:%270%27)),version:4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live on Godbolt</a></kbd></p>
<p>Clang doesn't optimize away a call to <code>DoSomethingElse</code>, but it does optimize away the deref of <code>ptr</code>!  It just leaves garbage in EDI as the function arg, despite not warning about it even with <code>-Wall</code>.  With <code>-fsanitize=undefined</code>, Clang does emit a call to an error function ahead of the call to <code>DoSomethingElse</code>, but still no compile-time warning.  (<a href="https://godbolt.org/#g:!((g:!((g:!((h:codeEditor,i:(filename:%271%27,fontScale:14,fontUsePx:%270%27,j:1,lang:c%2B%2B,selection:(endColumn:22,endLineNumber:13,positionColumn:22,positionLineNumber:13,selectionStartColumn:22,selectionStartLineNumber:13,startColumn:22,startLineNumber:13),source:%27int+*bar()%3B%0Abool+foo()%3B%0A%0Avoid+DoSomething(int)%3B%0Aint+dummy%3B%0Avoid+DoSomethingElse(int)%3B+//++%7Bdummy%3D1%3B+%7D%3B%0Avoid+DoYetAnotherThing()%3B%0A%0Aint+main()+%7B%0A++int*+ptr+%3D+bar()%3B%0A++bool+b+%3D+foo()%3B%0A%0A++if+(ptr+!!%3D+nullptr)%0A++++DoSomething(*ptr)%3B%0A++else+if+(b)%0A++++DoSomethingElse(*ptr)%3B%0A++else%0A++++DoYetAnotherThing()%3B%0A++++%0A++return+0%3B%0A%7D%27),l:%275%27,n:%270%27,o:%27C%2B%2B+source+%231%27,t:%270%27)),k:51.86279704452454,l:%274%27,m:100,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,s:0,t:%270%27),(g:!((g:!((h:compiler,i:(compiler:g161,filters:(b:%270%27,binary:%271%27,binaryObject:%271%27,commentOnly:%270%27,debugCalls:%271%27,demangle:%270%27,directives:%270%27,execute:%271%27,intel:%270%27,libraryCode:%270%27,trim:%271%27,verboseDemangling:%270%27),flagsViewOpen:%271%27,fontScale:14,fontUsePx:%270%27,j:1,lang:c%2B%2B,libs:!(),options:%27-O3+-Wall%27,overrides:!(),selection:(endColumn:1,endLineNumber:1,positionColumn:1,positionLineNumber:1,selectionStartColumn:1,selectionStartLineNumber:1,startColumn:1,startLineNumber:1),source:1),l:%275%27,n:%270%27,o:%27+x86-64+gcc+16.1+(Editor+%231)%27,t:%270%27)),k:48.137202955475466,l:%274%27,m:49.828175938674846,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,s:0,t:%270%27),(g:!((h:compiler,i:(compiler:clang2210,filters:(b:%270%27,binary:%271%27,binaryObject:%271%27,commentOnly:%270%27,debugCalls:%271%27,demangle:%270%27,directives:%270%27,execute:%271%27,intel:%270%27,libraryCode:%270%27,trim:%271%27,verboseDemangling:%270%27),flagsViewOpen:%271%27,fontScale:14,fontUsePx:%270%27,j:2,lang:c%2B%2B,libs:!(),options:%27-O3+-Wall+-Wextra+-fsanitize%3Dundefined%27,overrides:!(),selection:(endColumn:32,endLineNumber:22,positionColumn:32,positionLineNumber:22,selectionStartColumn:17,selectionStartLineNumber:22,startColumn:17,startLineNumber:22),source:1),l:%275%27,n:%270%27,o:%27+x86-64+clang+22.1.0+(Editor+%231)%27,t:%270%27)),header:(),l:%274%27,m:50.171824061325154,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,s:0,t:%270%27)),k:48.137202955475466,l:%273%27,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,t:%270%27)),l:%272%27,n:%270%27,o:%27%27,t:%270%27)),version:4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Godbolt</a>)</p>
<hr />
<p>Since you didn't specify the exact code or flags you used, I can only guess about why it failed to do this in your case. You do have to enable optimization, but I'd guess you already know that. It probably also won't happen if you write a dummy implementation of <code>DoSomethingElse</code> that never actually tries to use the parameter it's passed.</p>
<p>But clearly there are at least some circumstances when it can do what you're asking for.</p>
<p>I'd also agree with the comments saying you're usually prefer a warning of some sort. From a compiler, I'd consider that more of a &quot;nice to have&quot;, but from a lint, I'd consider it a much stronger expectation. And a quick test with pc-lint indicates that it does what I'd expect:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>14  if (ptr != nullptr)
15    DoSomething(*ptr);
16  else if (b)
17    DoSomethingElse(*ptr);

warning 413: likely use of null pointer 'ptr'
supplemental 831: when reversed, inference yields nullptr (line 14)
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://pclintplus.com/demo/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PC-Lint Plus Demo</a></p>
<p>Of course, there are a lot of lint tools, and I certainly haven't tried to check them all. Obviously at least one does catch it. I'd guess there are at least a few that don't, but can't say that with any kind of certainty--I'll openly admit that I pretty much stopped looking when the first one I tried caught it.</p>

Q: Laravel to send email for logging instead of writing to storage A: <p>Due Laravel uses the Monolog library internally, I think it is a better solution to use the intended log handlers. So every uncaught exception and manual calls of Log will be send to your mail address.</p>
<p><strong>if you're using mail()</strong></p>
<p>Add NativeMailerHandler to your app/start/global.php:</p>
<pre><code>$logFile = 'log-'.php_sapi_name().'.txt';

Log::useDailyFiles(storage_path().'/logs/'.$logFile);
Log::getMonolog()-&gt;pushHandler(
    new Monolog\Handler\NativeMailerHandler(
        'to@domain.de',
        '[Log] Some Subject',
        'from@domain.de'
        Logger::ERROR, // set minimal log lvl for mail
        true, // bubble to next handler?
        70 // max column width in your mail
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>if you've configured Laravel SMTP Mailing</strong></p>
<p>Add SwiftMailerHandler to your app/start/global.php:</p>
<p>(Swift Mailer is also used internally by Laravel)</p>
<pre><code>$logFile = 'log-'.php_sapi_name().'.txt';

Log::useDailyFiles(storage_path().'/logs/'.$logFile);
Log::getMonolog()-&gt;pushHandler(
    new Monolog\Handler\SwiftMailerHandler(
        Mail::getSwiftMailer(),
        Swift_Message::newInstance('[Log] Some Subject')-&gt;setFrom('from@domain.de')-&gt;setTo('to@domain.de'),
        Logger::ERROR, // set minimal log lvl for mail
        true // bubble to next handler?
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p>Add environment check as described in the other post if you want to. For many other handlers, also have a look at <a href="https://github.com/seldaek/monolog" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/seldaek/monolog</a></p>

Q: How do I get a radial gradient like this in flutter? A: <p>To expand on the accepted answer, start by breaking down the CSS into its constituent parts:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>background: radial-gradient(
  50% 50% at 50% 50%, 
  rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.116) 0%, 
  rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 100%
), #15636D;
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>50% 50% at 50% 50%</code> is the shape and position of the gradient, respectively. In this case, it means &quot;create a circle that is 50% of the width and height and center it at the point 50% of the width and height of the container&quot;. (As an aside, the second <code>50% 50%</code> could be simplified to just <code>center</code>.)</p>
<p>The <code>rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.116) 0%</code> sets a gradient stop at 0% - e.g. the center of the radial - to white at an 11.6% transparency.</p>
<p>The <code>rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 100%)</code> sets a gradient stop at 100% - e.g. the outer edge of the radial - to white at full transparency.</p>
<p>The trailing <code>#15636D</code> tells the background to set the backdrop of the radial gradient to that color, resulting in a semi-transparent white overlay over a solid color.</p>
<hr />
<p>The one-to-one conversion of these components to the Flutter equivalent would mean to simply recreate the components using Flutter counterparts:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>// Using these Color constructors to remain consistent with the CSS

const backdropColor = Color(0xFF15636D);

final gradientStartColor = Color.fromRGBO(255, 255, 255, 0.116);
final gradientEndColor = Color.fromRGBO(255, 255, 255, 0.0);

...

DecoratedBox(
  decoration: BoxDecoration(
    color: backdropColor,
  ),
  child: DecoratedBox(
    decoration: BoxDecoration(
      gradient: RadialGradient(
        center: Alignment(0, 0), // Corresponding to the 50% 50% position from the CSS
        radius: 0.5,             // Corresponding to the 50% 50% radius from the CSS
        colors: [
          gradientStartColor,
          gradientEndColor,
        ],
        stops: [
          0.0, // Position of start color, corresponding to the 0% from the CSS
          1.0, // Position of end color, corresponding to the 100% from the CSS
        ],
      ),
    ),
  ),
),
</code></pre>
<p>(Note: When all you're doing is providing a colored background to some or all of the screen, using <code>DecoratedBox</code> is better practice than using a <code>Container</code>.)</p>
<hr />
<p>However, one thing to note is that there will never be anything under the semi-transparent gradient except for the solid color. So what you can do instead is to use what's called a premultiplied alpha. Essentially, you combine the semi-transparent color with the solid color to just directly use the color that would get displayed anyway. This will save you memory and performance by eliminating unnecessary widgets from the tree and save your app from having to process transparent surfaces on the fly.</p>
<p>To get your premultiplied start color, all you need to do is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_interpolation" rel="nofollow noreferrer">linearly interpolate</a> from the solid color to the start color using the alpha value as the factor. Conveniently, Flutter already has a utility to do this for you with <a href="https://api.flutter.dev/flutter/dart-ui/Color/alphaBlend.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Color.alphaBlend</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>const backdropColor = Color(0xFF15636D);

final gradientStartColor = Color.fromRGBO(255, 255, 255, 0.116);
final gradientEndColor = Color.fromRGBO(255, 255, 255, 0.0);

final premultipliedStartColor = Color.alphaBlend(gradientStartColor, backdropColor);
final premultipliedEndColor = Color.alphaBlend(gradientEndColor, backdropColor);
</code></pre>
<p>Once you have those values, you can eliminate the outer <code>DecoratedBox</code> altogether and render the inner <code>DecoratedBox</code> with a fully opaque gradient:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>DecoratedBox(
  decoration: BoxDecoration(
    gradient: RadialGradient(
      center: Alignment(0, 0),
      radius: 0.5,
      colors: [
        premultipliedStartColor,
        premultipliedEndColor,
      ],
      stops: [
        0.0,
        1.0,
      ],
    ),
  ),
),
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>A few ways this can be further cleaned up is:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Alignment(0, 0)</code> corresponds to the center alignment. This can be more simply described as <code>Alignment.center</code>, or since Dart 3.10/Flutter 3.38, simply as <code>.center</code>.</li>
<li>The <code>stops</code> property is optional, and if it isn't defined, the stops of the colors will be uniformly distributed through the gradient. In the case of there being only two colors, this means that the first color will be put at 0.0 and the second color will be put at 1.0, which is exactly where we want them.</li>
<li>Because <code>gradientEndColor</code> is fully transparent, the returned value of the call to <code>Color.alphaBlend(gradientEndColor, backdropColor)</code> is identical to <code>backdropColor</code>, which makes the <code>premultipliedEndColor</code> entirely redundant - you can just use the <code>backdropColor</code> as the gradient's end color.</li>
<li>Mark as many things as <code>const</code> as possible. Because the values of the colors never change, it is better to pre-calculate them and then specify them as constants. This will allow you to make the <code>RadialGradient</code>, the <code>BoxDecoration</code>, and perhaps even the <code>DecoratedBox</code> constants as well (depending on the <code>DecoratedBox</code>'s children, if any).
<ul>
<li>A simple way to do this calculation is to run the above code and add <code>print('0x${premultipliedStartColor.toARGB32().toRadixString(16).padLeft(8, '0').toUpperCase()}');</code>. You can then take the printed value and use it as the value in your constant (which in this case is &quot;0xFF30757E&quot;).</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>With these cleanups, the final version is:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>const gradientStartColor = Color(0xFF30757E);
const gradientEndColor = Color(0xFF15636D);

...

// Put the const on this line if the DecoratedBox has const children or no children
const DecoratedBox( 
  // Otherwise, put the const on this line
  decoration: const BoxDecoration(
    gradient: RadialGradient(
      center: .center,
      radius: 0.5,
      colors: [
        gradientStartColor,
        gradientEndColor,
      ],
    ),
  ),
),
</code></pre>

Q: How to iterate over two objects without heap allocations? A: <p>You can cast a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/operators/collection-expressions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">collection expression</a> to <code>(Span&lt;Person&gt;)</code> and the compiler will use an internal struct with the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/proposals/csharp-12.0/inline-arrays" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>InlineArray</code> attribute</a>. This is entirely non-heap-allocating.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Person p1 = new();  
Person p2 = new();  
// ...
foreach (Person p in (Span&lt;Person&gt;)[p1, p2])
{
    p.DoSomething();
    p.DoMoreStuff();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://sharplab.io/#v2:CYLg1APgAgTAjAWAFBQMwAJboMLoN7LpGYZQAs6AsgBQD2ARgFYCmAxgC7oCWANOgyw7pGASkLECSYtPQAzWgCdmAQ1YALdNQBuyhegAe3AHaaASiuAB5IwBsAngGUADsqMAeAW3YA+EQG1eRgBdMSkZIklw8Kg4AE5qfREAbnFwgF9U9AykNKA=" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Sharplab.io</a></p>
<hr />
<p>To do this in older versions of C#, you can just create it yourself. You still need at least C# version 8, and .NET version 8 (and as far as I know it's not supported in Unity).</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>[InlineArray(2)]
private struct ArrayOf2&lt;T&gt;
{
    private T _element0;

    public ArrayOf2(T item0, T item1)
    {
        AsSpan()[0] = item0;
        AsSpan()[1] = item1;
    }

    public Span&lt;T&gt; AsSpan() =&gt;
        MemoryMarshal.CreateSpan&lt;T&gt;(ref _element0, 2);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Alternately use a struct with fields, and <code>SequentialLayout</code> then get a span off that.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>[StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
private struct ArrayOf2&lt;T&gt;
{
    private T _element0;
    private T _element1;

    public ArrayOf2(T item0, T item1)
    {
        _element0 = item0;
        _element1 = item1;
    }

    public Span&lt;T&gt; AsSpan() =&gt;
        MemoryMarshal.CreateSpan&lt;T&gt;(ref _element0, 2);
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Person p1 = new();  
Person p2 = new();  
// ...
foreach (Person p in new ArrayOf2&lt;Person&gt;(p1, p2).AsSpan())
{
    p.DoSomething();
    p.DoMoreStuff();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://sharplab.io/#v2:" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Sharplab.io</a></p>
